from pathlib import Path
import json
import pandas as pd
BASE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
s=json.loads((BASE/'results/summary.json').read_text())
sc=pd.read_csv(BASE/'results/oos_scorecard.csv').set_index('model')
reg=pd.read_csv(BASE/'results/regime_scores.csv').set_index('regime')
alt=pd.read_csv(BASE/'results/horizon_lag_sensitivity.csv')
fmt=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}'
status='negative incremental forecast' if s['rmse_improvement']<0 else 'positive incremental but unproven'
sections=[
('01. Abstract',f'''**Primary research question:** Does a two-month-stale manufacturing-minus-auto momentum gap predict three-month-ahead U.S. motor-vehicle-and-parts output growth beyond simple and multivariate baselines? In a 2026-10-16 revised G.17 snapshot, **{s['raw_months']} monthly observations** yield **{s['oos_origins']} purged expanding-window OOS forecasts** ({s['first_origin']} to {s['last_origin']}). The augmented model's RMSE is **{fmt(sc.loc['augmented','rmse'])} log-pp**, versus **{fmt(sc.loc['macro_control','rmse'])}** for controls and **{fmt(sc.loc['mean','rmse'])}** for the historical mean; incremental improvement is **{fmt(s['rmse_improvement'])} log-pp** (negative means worse). **Verdict:** {status}; **REPRODUCED RETROSPECTIVE MACRO PILOT — NOT PIT / NOT EQUITY ALPHA**. The 201 automobile observations were manually transcribed and only 12 spot-checked; first-release historical vintages and securities returns are unavailable.'''),
('02. Research Design & Hypotheses','''**H1:** a positive manufacturing–auto momentum premium signals delayed automotive catch-up. **H2:** adding the fixed nonlinear factor improves OOS RMSE versus a lagged auto/manufacturing control, and must also be compared with historical mean and persistence. **H3:** any incremental improvement survives block-aware placebos, bootstrap uncertainty, scale/momentum-mix perturbations, ablations, year deletions, regime splits, and alternative horizons. The null is no incremental predictive value; failure against the historical mean or instability rejects a useful signal. Mechanism: shared business-cycle orders and supplier constraints could lead auto output; counterthesis: idiosyncratic shutdowns, strikes and model transitions dominate.'''),
('03. Industry Context','''The dependent series is U.S. **NAICS 3361–3363** motor vehicles and parts, seasonally adjusted physical industrial production, index 2017=100. Aggregate U.S. manufacturing production (`IPMAN`) is the comparator; it includes the auto industry, creating mechanical overlap. Auto manufacturing is highly cyclical and susceptible to inventory, chip shortages, strikes, and plant closures; a positive macro gap can equally mean automotive weakness rather than catch-up. The Fed's **2026-10-16 G.17** release reported September manufacturing output rose 0.3%; no unsupported industry market-size estimate is inserted. Official context: [G.17](https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g17/Current/default.htm), [NAICS](https://www.census.gov/naics/).'''),
('04. Data & Source Integrity','''**A-grade**: Fed G.17 `IPG3361T3S` auto index and `IPMAN` manufacturing index (2010-01–2026-09, 2017=100, monthly SA); G.17 release dated **2026-10-16**. Auto index values were manually transcribed from the [official FRED table](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/data/IPG3361T3S) on 2026-10-27 and **12 of 201** were spot-checked, not all independently machine-verified. IPMAN was inherited from the prior Basic Chemicals frozen study; the full 201 values were not independently re-downloaded. **B-grade**: FRED metadata and ALFRED archival-vintage discovery. **C-grade**: Census 2022 NAICS classification. `data/source_register.csv` gives exact URLs, dates, observed windows, grades, revision notes, release lags, and roles. Missing observations were not imputed; all index values are positive.'''),
('05. Point-in-Time Availability','''At formation month `t`, every signal/control uses an observation no later than **t−2**. Target is motor-vehicle output YoY growth at **t+3**; the model trains only on historical examples whose target month is **≤t−2**. This yields a conservative five-month origin gap for the primary horizon, but historical Fed first-publication dates and revised index values are **UNKNOWN**. Therefore this is a retrospective fixed-snapshot exercise, **not a verified point-in-time strategy**. The Fed explicitly revises historical G.17 readings; the lag alone cannot repair vintage leakage.'''),
('06. Factor Construction','''Let `A_t` be auto IP and `M_t` aggregate manufacturing IP. Define `g^k_{Z,t}=100[ln(Z_t)-ln(Z_{t-k})]`. **Fixed primary factor:** `F_t = tanh((g^3_{M,t-2} - g^3_{A,t-2}) / 10)`. **Target:** `Y_t=g^12_{A,t+3}` in log percentage points. Primary scale 10 and 3-month weight 1 are fixed, not selected on OOS results. For perturbations, replace the numerator with `w*(g^3_M-g^3_A)+(1-w)*(g^12_M-g^12_A)`, with `w∈[0.6,1]` and tanh scale `5–20`; the primary remains `w=1,scale=10`. Model standardization is estimated on training data only.'''),
('07. Exposure & Transmission','''`data/exposure_map.csv` maps the industry to NAICS 3361 (vehicles), 3362 (bodies/trailers) and 3363 (parts), based on the [2022 Census NAICS](https://www.census.gov/naics/). FY2025 [GM 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467858/000146785826000013/gm-20251231.htm) (filed 2026-01-27) and [Ford 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/37996/000003799626000015/f-20251231.htm) (filed 2026-02-11) identify **qualitative** automotive company exposure. Their U.S. physical production weights and historic changes are UNKNOWN; no invented company exposure coefficients enter the factor. The proposed channel is broad manufacturing momentum → auto supply/production catch-up, not a demonstrated causal chain. **Firm fundamentals, leave-one-company-out and total returns: NOT TESTED.**'''),
('08. Outcomes & Baselines','''All models use identical forecast origins and a **purged expanding training window** (minimum 60 eligible origins), Ridge alpha=12 and train-only feature scaling. Baselines: expanding historical mean; auto YoY persistence; macro controls (lagged auto YoY, lagged manufacturing YoY, lagged auto 3-month momentum); factor-only Ridge; augmented controls plus the nonlinear factor. Outcomes are industrial production YoY, not firm revenue/EPS, stock prices or adjusted total returns. RMSE/MAE are in log-pp; time-series Spearman correlation is descriptive and **not** cross-sectional stock IC.'''),
('09. Empirical Findings',f'''**Same-origin OOS scorecard (n={s['oos_origins']}):**\n\n| Model | RMSE log-pp | MAE log-pp | Time-series Spearman |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n'''+ '\n'.join(f"| {model} | {fmt(sc.loc[model,'rmse'])} | {fmt(sc.loc[model,'mae'])} | {fmt(sc.loc[model,'rank_ic'])} |" for model in ['mean','persistence','macro_control','factor_only','augmented'])+f'''\n\n**Incremental RMSE improvement:** {fmt(s['rmse_improvement'])} log-pp (macro-control minus augmented). The large 2020 auto shutdown and 2021 rebound inflate squared-error metrics; compare pre/post periods rather than generalizing from pooled RMSE. The scorecard is calculated from `results/oos_predictions.csv` and reproduced in the executed notebook.'''),
('10. Robustness & Placebos',f'''**1,000** 12-month contiguous-block factor reorders with full purged model refits produce an exploratory one-sided tail fraction of **{s['placebo_tail_fraction']:.4f}**, not an exact exchangeable permutation p-value. **2,000** paired circular moving-block bootstrap draws (block 12) yield an OOS RMSE-improvement 95% interval **[{fmt(s['bootstrap_95'][0])}, {fmt(s['bootstrap_95'][1])}] log-pp**. **1,000** nonlinear-scale/momentum-mix perturbations improve the control in **{100*s['sensitivity_positive_fraction']:.1f}%** of cases. Ablations, leave-one-calendar-year-out and 16 horizon/lag variants are in `results/`. No firm-level leave-one-company-out is possible. Multiple-testing adjustment is absent: all robustness comparisons are exploratory.'''),
('11. Regime Sensitivity',f'''With the **same originally fitted forecasts**, RMSE improvement is **{fmt(reg.loc['pre_2020','improvement'])}** log-pp before 2020 (n={int(reg.loc['pre_2020','n'])}), **{fmt(reg.loc['pandemic_2020_2021','improvement'])}** in 2020–21 (n={int(reg.loc['pandemic_2020_2021','n'])}), **{fmt(reg.loc['post_2022','improvement'])}** after 2022 (n={int(reg.loc['post_2022','n'])}), and **{fmt(reg.loc['excluding_2020_2021','improvement'])}** with pandemic origins excluded (n={int(reg.loc['excluding_2020_2021','n'])}). Horizons 1/3/6/12 months and observation lags 1/2/3/4 months are recomputed in `results/horizon_lag_sensitivity.csv`; leave-one-calendar-year-out is `results/leave_one_year_out.csv`. A year deletion is not a company deletion.'''),
('12. Bias Audit & Verdict','''**Publication/vintage leakage:** unresolved; 2026-revised data cannot support PIT. **Lookahead:** mechanical 2-month lag and target purge implemented, but not sufficient for vintage validity. **Survivorship:** no security universe; qualitative FY2025 company examples are not backfilled. **Selection/multiple tests:** one of many industry hypotheses, with exploratory 16 horizon/lag checks and no false-discovery correction. **Nonstationarity:** 2020 shutdown, auto supply shortages, strikes and EV transition. **Costs/capacity/benchmark-adjusted total returns:** NOT TESTED. **Verdict:** a negative, reproducible retrospective **macro-leading-indicator falsification**, not firm-fundamental prediction or investable equity alpha.'''),
('13. Reproduction','''From this study directory after installing `requirements.txt`, run:\n\n```bash\nOPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python run_analysis.py\nPYTHONPATH=. OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -m pytest -q tests\nOPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute --inplace research.ipynb\n```\n\nThe full analysis recomputes 1,000 block reorders, 2,000 bootstraps and 1,000 perturbations. `research.ipynb` recomputes the baseline forecasts and embeds saved tables/plots. `make_data.py` reconstructs the manually transcribed auto series from its source-code literals and the included inherited frozen manufacturing CSV; it is not an official machine download. `logs/` records actual execution and clean-copy reproduction; `scripts/validate_studies.py` must be run **from the full repository root** to check indexed-study layout.'''),
('14. Next-Stage Protocol','''(1) Programmatically re-download all 201 official auto/manufacturing rows and verify hashes/values; (2) reconstruct archival ALFRED/Fed monthly release vintages and exact release timestamps; (3) obtain historical OEM/model-level production, supplier shipments, inventory, strikes and chip-shortage data; (4) map contemporaneous SEC filing dates to firm revenue and margins with a historical exposure matrix; (5) only after these pass, evaluate delisting-adjusted total returns, sector/market benchmarks, trading costs/capacity, clean holdout and multiple-testing corrections. Pre-register sign, horizon and universe before new collection; do not tune away the current negative result.'''),
('15. Sources & Artifacts','''Official [G.17 2026-10-16](https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g17/Current/default.htm); [FRED auto table](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/data/IPG3361T3S); [FRED manufacturing table](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/data/IPMAN); [GM SEC FY2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1467858/000146785826000013/gm-20251231.htm); [Ford SEC FY2025](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/37996/000003799626000015/f-20251231.htm); [Census NAICS](https://www.census.gov/naics/); [ALFRED archive](https://alfred.stlouisfed.org/). See `data/source_register.csv` and `data/source_qa.md` for precise source roles, grades, publication dates, observation ranges and UNKNOWN vintage fields. Artifacts: frozen raw/derived CSVs, `results/` full diagnostics, `figures/` 11 calculated SVG charts, `tests/` unit checks, `logs/` execution evidence, and the 49-cell executed `research.ipynb`.''')]
assert len(sections)==15
out='# Manufacturing Catch-up Gap → U.S. Motor Vehicles & Parts Production\n\n**2026-10-27 | REPRODUCED RETROSPECTIVE MACRO PILOT — NOT PIT / NOT EQUITY ALPHA**\n\n'
for title,body in sections:out+=f'## {title}\n\n{body}\n\n'
(BASE/'README.md').write_text(out)
manifest={'schema_version':1,'id':'manufacturing-catchup-gap-us-motor-vehicles-parts-2026-10-27','title':'Manufacturing Catch-up Gap → U.S. Motor Vehicles & Parts Production','industry':'U.S. motor vehicles and parts manufacturing NAICS 3361–3363','research_status':'REPRODUCED RETROSPECTIVE MACRO PILOT — NOT PIT / NOT EQUITY ALPHA','equity_alpha_established':False,'point_in_time_verified':False,'firm_level_fundamentals_tested':False,'equity_total_returns_tested':False,'notebook':'research.ipynb','analysis_entrypoint':'run_analysis.py','requirements':'requirements.txt','source_register':'data/source_register.csv','notes_file':'data/source_qa.md','data_files':[str(p.relative_to(BASE)) for p in sorted((BASE/'data').iterdir()) if p.is_file()],'result_files':[str(p.relative_to(BASE)) for folder in ['results','figures'] for p in sorted((BASE/folder).iterdir()) if p.is_file()],'test_commands':['OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python run_analysis.py','PYTHONPATH=. OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python -m pytest -q tests','OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute --inplace research.ipynb','python scripts/validate_studies.py (from repository root)'],'n_raw':s['raw_months'],'n_oos':s['oos_origins'],'period':'2010-01 to 2026-09','date_prepared':'2026-10-27','source_vintage':'G17 2026-10-16 revised fixed snapshot, historic release vintages UNKNOWN'}
(BASE/'study.json').write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2)+'\n')
summary=f'''The two-month-stale nonlinear manufacturing–auto momentum gap was tested against three-month-ahead U.S. motor-vehicle-and-parts production growth using {s['oos_origins']} purged expanding-window OOS forecasts from the revised 2026-10-16 G.17 snapshot. Adding the factor worsened RMSE from {fmt(sc.loc['macro_control','rmse'])} to {fmt(sc.loc['augmented','rmse'])} log-pp, and the historical-mean baseline achieved {fmt(sc.loc['mean','rmse'])} log-pp. A 1,000-refit 12-month block-reorder placebo yielded an exploratory tail fraction {s['placebo_tail_fraction']:.3f}, while the 2,000-draw paired block-bootstrap interval [{fmt(s['bootstrap_95'][0])}, {fmt(s['bootstrap_95'][1])}] log-pp and pandemic-exclusion checks did not rescue the signal. **Verdict:** a reproducible negative retrospective macro-production pilot, with manually transcribed and incompletely audited rows, no historical PIT vintages, no company fundamentals and no tested equity total-return alpha.'''
(BASE/'root_readme_additions.md').write_text('## Research Index — new row\n\n| [Manufacturing Catch-up Gap — U.S. Motor Vehicles & Parts](Manufacturing-Catchup-Gap-to-Auto-Production-U.S.-Motor-Vehicles/) | Two-month-stale manufacturing vs auto momentum → 3m-ahead auto output YoY | Negative incremental OOS; not PIT / equity alpha | [Open](Manufacturing-Catchup-Gap-to-Auto-Production-U.S.-Motor-Vehicles/research.ipynb) |\n\n## Research Summaries — exactly 4 sentences\n\n### [Manufacturing Catch-up Gap — U.S. Motor Vehicles & Parts](Manufacturing-Catchup-Gap-to-Auto-Production-U.S.-Motor-Vehicles/)\n\n'+summary+'\n')
print('README',len(out),'chars; summary sentences',summary.count('. ')+1,'manifest files',len(manifest['data_files']),len(manifest['result_files']))
